# LaTeX Format Rules for .tex files

paths:
  - "**/*.tex"

---

## ABSOLUTE PROHIBITIONS

- NO Markdown syntax in .tex files: `**bold**`, `*italic*`, `- list`, `1. numbered`
- NO `\bm` for vectors/matrices — use `\mathbf{}` or `\boldsymbol{}`
- NO `\I` for indicator — use `\mathbb{I}`
- NO Unicode subscripts like `n₁` — use `$n_1$`
- NO `\tag{}` or hardcoded equation numbers like `式 (★)` — use `\label{}` + `\cref{}`
- NO Obsidian callout blocks like `> [!note]`, `> [!example]`, `> [!thm]`
- NO `\ref{}` — must use `\cref{}` for all references

## REQUIRED

- Lists: `\begin{enumerate}...\end{enumerate}` or `\begin{itemize}...\end{itemize}`
- Bold text: `\textbf{}`
- Italic text: `\textit{}`
- References: `\label{}` + `\cref{}` (NOT bare `\ref{}`)
- Indicator function: `\mathbb{I}`
- Theorem/Definition inside: NO itemize — use enumerate with semicolons

## LABEL & REFERENCE RULES

Label naming conventions:
- Definitions/Theorems: `\label{def:NameXX}`
- Lemmas: `\label{def:LemmaXX}`
- Sections: `\label{sec:name}`
- Equations: `\label{eq:name}`
- Figures: `\label{fig:descriptive-name}`
- Exercises: `\label{exr:chapter-num}`

**Examples:**
```latex
\begin{Theorem}[Neyman's Theorem]\label{def:NeymanTheorem}
...
\end{Theorem}
见 \cref{def:NeymanTheorem}。

\begin{equation}\label{eq:ATE}
\tau = \bar{Y}(1) - \bar{Y}(0)
\end{equation}
见 \cref{eq:ATE}。
```

## EXERCISE FORMAT

### Book Template (do Carmo / geometry notes)
```latex
\begin{exercise}{1-2, 1 — do Carmo, Exercise 1-2, 1}
Find a parametrized curve $\alpha(t)$ whose trace is the circle...
\end{exercise}
```
- Format: `{section, num — do Carmo, Exercise section, num}`
- Hard problems: mark with `*` like `{1-3, 8* — do Carmo, ...}`
- Use `\begin{enumerate}` for sub-parts

### Causal Inference Template (Peng Ding)
```latex
\begin{Exercise}{\ref{exr:5-1} Covariate balance in the CRE}\label{exr:5-1}
证明 \eqref{eq:balance-discrete-CRE}：在 CRE 下，...
\end{Exercise}
```
- Format: `{\ref{exr:chapter-num} English Title}\label{exr:chapter-num}`
- Label: `exr:{chapter}-{number}`
- MUST use `\eqref{}` to reference textbook equation numbers
- English title from textbook

## MATH SYMBOLS (User Conventions)

| Concept | Symbol | Command |
|---------|--------|---------|
| Probability | P | `\mathbb{P}(A)` |
| Expectation (single var) | E | `\mathbb{E}X` (no parentheses) |
| Expectation (multiple var) | E | `\mathbb{E}(XY)` (with parentheses) |
| Variance | var | `\text{var}(X)` |
| Covariance | cov | `\text{cov}(X,Y)` |
| Correlation | corr | `\text{corr}(X,Y)` |
| Indicator | I | `\mathbb{I}` |
| p-value | p | `\text{p}_{}` (empty subscript) |
| Independence | Perp | `$A \Perp B$` |

## SYMBOL FIRST APPEARANCE RULE

When a symbol appears for the first time, you MUST:
1. Define its meaning in the text
2. Use standard LaTeX: `\sqrt{}`, `\frac{}{}`, etc.
3. Convention: hat (^) = sample estimate, no hat = population truth

## PROOF ENVIRONMENT

```latex
\newtheorem*{Proof}{证明}
\begin{Proof}[Optional Title]
...proof content...
\end{Proof}
```

## EXAMPLE ENVIRONMENT (Motivation-First Principle)

| Type | Location | Purpose |
|------|----------|---------|
| Motivation | BEFORE concept definition | Show why the concept is needed |
| Application | AFTER concept definition | Show how to use the concept |
| Demonstration | AFTER theorem | Show real-world application |

## APPENDIX CITATION

Detailed derivations go in appendix. Reference from main text:
```latex
...theorem statement...\footnote{推导见附录 \cref{sec:appendix-derivation}。}
```
